{%- set cr = credentials.en -%}

<section class="about-hero">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="label">About the coach</span>
    <h1>Iván Koch</h1>
    <p class="about-lede">I have coached endurance athletes for more than three years, in Spanish, English and Portuguese. Before I coached anyone, I raced 20 marathons and finished 3 Ironmans. Both matter: I know what the data says, and I know what hour four feels like.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="about-record">
  <div class="wrap">
    <ul class="record-grid">
      {%- for r in cr.record %}
      <li>
        <span class="record-figure">{{ r.figure | replace("{reviewCount}", site.reviewCount) }}</span>
        <span class="record-label">{{ r.label }}</span>
        <span class="record-note">{{ r.note }}</span>
      </li>
      {%- endfor %}
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="wrap about-prose">
  <section>
    <h2>How I got here</h2>
    <p>I started as a rower as a kid, moved to running, and ended up in triathlon and trail. Twenty marathons later my personal best is under 3 hours, and of the three full Ironmans I have finished, the fastest was 10:06.</p>
    <p>Triaperformance started with one friend who wanted to run a marathon. It is now more than 100 athletes coached, several of them sub-3 marathoners and full-Ironman finishers. I did not stop racing in order to coach — I still train and still race, and that is part of why the method holds up.</p>
    <p>Outside sport I work in operations. That explains more about how I coach than it sounds like it should: I measure, I compare against a plan, and I change course when the data says to — not when the week got hard.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How I coach</h2>
    <p>One service, no tiers and no fine print. Testing before I write your first week, zones built from your numbers rather than a generic table, feedback every Monday without fail, adjustments whenever you ask for them, and a re-test to prove the progress is real instead of a feeling.</p>
    <p>My athletes describe that service better than I do: adapting to their schedule and the weekly check-in are the two themes that come up most in the reviews.</p>
    <p><a href="/en/training-zones-calculator/">Try the zones calculator</a> — it is public, it is the same one I use with my athletes, and it shows you concretely how I work before you ever talk to me.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="trainingpeaks">
    <h2>TrainingPeaks</h2>
    <p>I am a TrainingPeaks partner and a Level 2 Accredited coach on their platform. In practice that means two things.</p>
    <p>First: TrainingPeaks reviews and accredits the coaches on its platform, and that accreditation is renewed every year. Second, and this is the part that matters to you: TrainingPeaks maintains a <strong>limited directory</strong> of coaches — Coach Match — which you enter by application and review. When an athlete fills in the questionnaire, TrainingPeaks reads the answers and refers them to a coach by fit. They refer athletes to me from across Latin America and beyond.</p>
    <p>Put plainly: this is not me saying I am a good coach. It is the platform most of the world's endurance coaches work on deciding, case by case, to send me athletes.</p>
    <p>I also publish my full training plan catalogue there — {{ plans.byLanguage.en.length }} in English, and more than 300 across all three languages.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Certifications</h2>
    <ul class="cred-list">
      {%- for item in cr.items %}
      <li{% if item.badge %} class="has-badge"{% endif %}>
        {%- if item.badge %}
        <img class="cred-badge" src="{{ item.badge | v }}" alt="{{ item.badgeAlt }}" width="56" height="56" decoding="async">
        {%- endif %}
        <span class="cred-text">
          <span class="cred-name">{{ item.name }}</span>
          <span class="cred-issuer">{{ item.issuer }}</span>
          <span class="cred-note">{{ item.note }}</span>
        </span>
      </li>
      {%- endfor %}
    </ul>
  </section>
</div>

